%[text] # How can we detect coins?
%[text] Our goal is to automatically detect the coins in an image and find out how many there are. Before we write a detection algorithm, let us look at the image and develop some ideas.
%%
%[text] ## Open the image
%[text] Run this section to open the example image coins.png, which comes with Image Processing Toolbox. No image download is needed.
%[text] The first command reads the image and stores it in a variable called coinImage. A variable gives a name to data so we can use it again. The second command displays that image. The semicolons keep the image data from being printed as numbers.
coinImage = imread("coins.png");
imageshow(coinImage); %[output:32b6a22b]
%%
%[text] ## Look first
%[text] How many coins can you count? 
%[text] How do you recognise the coins? Which clues help you decide where one coin ends and the background begins?
%%
%[text] ## How could a computer detect the coins?
%[text] Discuss this question with a neighbour: how could we tell a computer which parts of this image are coins?
%[text] Think about what makes the coins different from the background:
%[text] - Brightness: do coins and background look equally bright? Is every part of a coin equally bright?
%[text] - Shape: what shapes do you notice, and could shape help us find a coin?
%[text] - Size: how could size help distinguish a coin from a small mark?
%[text] - Separation: how can we tell where one coin ends and another begins? What might happen if two coins touch? \
%%
%[text] ## Suggest a detection rule
%[text] Write down one rule you would ask a computer to follow. Start with: "I would identify a coin by ..."
%[text] Then describe one situation in which your rule might fail. Consider a shadow, a bright background, a different camera angle, or coins touching each other.
%[text] Share your idea with the group. There can be more than one useful approach; explain what your approach assumes about the image.

